@node Language Conformance Exclusions & Proposed Syntax
@chapter Language Conformance Exclusions & Proposed Syntax

This chapter documents, for each of the 17 EIGHTBOL language frontends, which canonical AST node types are supported, which are excluded (with justification), and proposed syntax for any missing constructs.

@section Canonical AST Node Set (Complete Reference)

@strong{Arithmetic:} @code{:+}, @code{:-}, @code{:×}, @code{:÷}

@strong{Conditions:} @code{=}, @code{≠}, @code{<}, @code{≤}, @code{>}, @code{≥}

@strong{Logical:} @code{:and}, @code{:or}, @code{¬}

@strong{Bitwise:} @code{:bit-and}, @code{:bit-or}, @code{:bit-xor}, @code{:bit-not}, @code{(:ash :value @var{v} :shift @var{n})}

@strong{Control Flow:} @code{:if}, @code{:perform}, @code{:goto}, @code{:evaluate}

@strong{Calls:} @code{:call}, @code{:invoke}, @code{:method}, @code{:assembly-entry}

@strong{Data:} @code{:move}, @code{:subscript}, @code{:refmod}, @code{:of}, @code{:address-of}

@strong{Strings:} @code{:string-blt}, @code{:string-length}, @code{:string-find}

@strong{Special:} @code{:log-fault}, @code{:debug-break}, @code{:inspect}, @code{:goback}, @code{:null}, @code{:self}

@strong{Data definitions:} @code{:dd} (from copybook)

---

@section 1. COBOL

@subsection Supported Constructs
All canonical constructs fully supported.

@subsection Excluded Constructs
None. COBOL maps cleanly to canonical AST.

---

@section 2. BASIC

@subsection Supported Constructs
@code{:+}, @code{:-}, @code{:×}, @code{:÷}, @code{=}, @code{≠}, @code{<}, @code{≤}, @code{>}, @code{≥}, @code{:and}, @code{:or}, @code{¬}, @code{:if}, @code{:perform}, @code{:call}, @code{:move}, @code{:subscript}, @code{:of}, @code{:goback}

@subsection Excluded Constructs
@strong{PRINT/INPUT statements:} BASIC's @code{PRINT} and @code{INPUT} compile to library function calls, not AST nodes. Justification: BASIC's I/O is stream-based and model-agnostic; no single AST representation fits all backends (some have screens, some have serial I/O). Resolution: @code{PRINT expr} → @code{(:call :target "PrintRoutine" :library t)}.

@strong{SET keyword:} BASIC's @code{SET} is synonymous with @code{LET}; both map to @code{:move}. Justification: Redundant syntax for single semantic construct.

@subsection Proposed Syntax for Missing Constructs
@strong{Null testing:} @code{IF NULL(var)} or @code{IF var IS NULL} → @code{(:null var)}

@strong{Self reference:} @code{SELF} keyword in object-oriented BASIC → @code{:self}

---

