@node Summary: Exclusion Justifications

@section Summary: Why Constructs Are Excluded

All exclusions fall into one of three categories:

@enumerate
@item @strong{Language artifact:} Feature is syntactic sugar or redundant (e.g., Pascal @code{WITH}, Smalltalk cascades). @emph{Resolution:} Parser expands to canonical constructs.

@item @strong{System limitation:} Feature requires runtime capabilities incompatible with 8-bit/16-bit systems (e.g., reflection, exceptions, late binding). @emph{Resolution:} Omit; user implements via library routines if needed.

@item @strong{Backend agnostic:} Feature is backend-specific and has no universal AST representation (e.g., PRINT/INPUT, SCUMM game engine). @emph{Resolution:} Compile to library calls + variable assignments; backend-specific code generation.

@end enumerate

No canonical AST construct is excluded. All 17 frontends converge to the same canonical form.

